<section class="ihero"><div class="ihero-text"><div class="ihero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span><a href="/appraisal-practice/">Services</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span><span aria-current="page">Earthquake Endorsement Claims</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Earthquake Claim Appraisal for Alaska Property Owners</h1><p class="lede">Alaska is the most seismically active state in the country. If you carry earthquake coverage, the November 2018 Anchorage earthquake showed how much disagreement there can be about the cost of repair.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line light" href="/contact-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Requires</dt><dd>Earthquake coverage</dd></div><div><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>Often a percentage</dd></div><div><dt>Common damage</dt><dd>Foundation, drywall</dd></div></dl></div></div><figure class="ihero-photo"><img src="/assets/img/photos/quake-damage.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/quake-damage-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/quake-damage.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 55vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></figure></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>Earthquake coverage is separate</h2>
<p>Standard homeowner and commercial property forms usually exclude earth movement, including earthquake. Coverage typically comes from an endorsement or separate policy, and it usually carries a percentage deductible tied to the coverage limit, often far higher than the regular deductible. Our <a href="/appraisal-tools/deductible-impact-calculator/">deductible impact calculator</a> shows what that means in dollars.</p>
<h2>What quake damage looks like</h2>
<ul>
<li>Cracked drywall and plaster, often throughout the house</li>
<li>Foundation cracking, settlement and slab damage</li>
<li>Chimney and masonry damage</li>
<li>Broken water lines, water heaters and fixtures</li>
<li>Doors and windows that no longer close properly</li>
<li>Damage to contents and built-ins</li>
</ul>
<h2>Why earthquake claims get disputed</h2>
<p>Cosmetic cracking and structural damage can look similar. Carriers may attribute cracks to settlement, frost heave or age rather than the quake. And because the deductible is high, small differences in the estimate determine whether any payment is owed at all. Pre-quake photos, inspection reports and engineering evaluations become very important.</p>
<h2>Aftershocks and multiple events</h2>
<p>Large Alaska quakes are followed by thousands of aftershocks. Policies differ on how they treat damage from a sequence of events, which affects how many deductibles apply. Read your endorsement carefully.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Earth movement versus other causes</b>Ground settlement, landslides and permafrost thaw are typically excluded from standard policies and may not be covered by an earthquake endorsement either. Coverage questions like these are best discussed with an attorney.</div>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Does my homeowner policy cover earthquakes?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Usually not. Earthquake coverage is generally added by endorsement or a separate policy. Check your declarations page.</p></div></details><details><summary>Why is my earthquake deductible so high?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Earthquake deductibles are typically a percentage of the dwelling limit, so a 10% deductible on a $400,000 limit is $40,000. Figures here are illustrative.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal resolve whether cracks came from the quake?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Appraisal values the loss. Whether some damage was caused by the earthquake can overlap with coverage, and how far a panel can go varies, so get advice if causation is the main issue.</p></div></details></div><h2>Related services</h2><div class="rel-grid"><a class="rel" href="/appraisal-practice/remote-property-claim-appraisal/"><small>Service</small><span>Remote &amp; Off-Road-System Properties</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel" href="/appraisal-practice/carrier-estimate-review/"><small>Service</small><span>Carrier Estimate Review</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel" href="/appraisal-practice/neutral-umpire-services/"><small>Service</small><span>Neutral Umpire Services</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6